# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/

# Build artefacts from an editable install
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# Secrets. Since slice D these live IN this tree, not in ~/.hermes/.env: ./.env
# for the pipeline and api/.env for the service, which holds a deliberately
# different, restricted credential. Both are mode 600 with a tracked
# .env.example beside them. This repository is cloned to several machines and
# hosted remotely: a credential committed here is a credential on all of them,
# and in the host's history permanently.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
secrets.json
# T-28: the contributor worker reads a config.json beside itself, and that file
# holds a minted API key. Not a .env, so the rules above miss it; scoped to the
# one directory because config.json is an ordinary name elsewhere.
backend/api/contributor-worker/config.json
credentials.json

# cloudflared. `cloudflared tunnel create` writes ~/.cloudflared/<UUID>.json,
# which is a BEARER CREDENTIAL for the tunnel -- anyone holding it can run the
# tunnel and receive traffic for its hostnames. cert.pem from `cloudflared
# login` is already covered by *.pem above. Neither belongs in deploy/, and
# deploy/cloudflared/config.yml points at their real home outside the repo;
# these two lines exist so that copying one in "just to test" cannot commit it.
deploy/**/*.json
.cloudflared/

# Local database artefacts
*.sql
*.sql.gz
*.dump
backup*.tar.gz

# Logs and scratch
*.log
tmp/

# Cached model responses (backend/evals). Large, worthless to anyone else, and
# they hold the full text of postings -- not something to put in git history by
# accident. evals/cache.py also drops a .gitignore in the directory itself, so
# a relocated EVAL_CACHE_DIR stays covered too.
evals/.cache/
backend/evals/.cache/

# Cached SERP responses (backend/serp, task 23). Same argument one word over:
# they are third-party job postings fetched from a metered account. serp/cache.py
# writes its own .gitignore too, so a relocated SERP_CACHE_DIR stays covered.
serp/.cache/
backend/serp/.cache/

# Agent worktrees and scratch state. CLAUDE.md is the deliberate exception: it
# is the project brief every session reads before doing anything, and while it
# was ignored it was machine-local -- so a correction to it could be made and
# could not be committed, which is how tasks 38 and 40 found themselves editing
# a file no diff could carry. settings.local.json stays ignored; it is this
# machine's permission state and means nothing on another.
#
# T-5/T-6/T-7 (TASKS.md) added four more directories in the same category as
# CLAUDE.md: rules/, agents/ and hooks/ are the harness itself -- landmines,
# read-only reviewers, the citation-audit hook -- not one machine's state, and
# settings.json is what wires the hook in. Un-ignoring only these four paths,
# not `.claude/*` wholesale, keeps everything else (scratch, worktrees,
# per-machine permission state) exactly as machine-local as it already was.
.claude/*
!.claude/CLAUDE.md
!.claude/rules/
!.claude/agents/
!.claude/hooks/
!.claude/settings.json

# Agent-orchestration harness, not project code. 183b4dc untracked
# scripts/tranche-two-launcher.sh on purpose -- it hard-codes this machine,
# drives tmux, and runs tranche_two tasks that are all done -- and 3383f9a said
# it would. It was NOT deleted: it is the owner's file and the owner's call
# whether it moves to ~/bin/. But untracked-and-unignored means it shows as ??
# in every git status and it has already been re-added once by a git add -A,
# which is the exact thing 183b4dc exists to undo. Ignoring it makes the
# decision durable instead of dependent on nobody typing -A.
#
# ANCHORED TO THE ROOT, and the anchor is the fix, not decoration. Written as a
# bare `scripts/` this pattern matches at EVERY depth, so it also ignored
# backend/scripts/ -- a directory with four tracked files in it. Tracked files
# are unaffected by .gitignore, so nothing broke and nothing was red; what broke
# was the NEXT file added there. Task 33 added backend/scripts/backup-jobs.sh
# and `git status` did not show it. A backup script that is silently not in the
# repo is the same failure mode as a backup that is silently not running.
#   Verify both halves after editing:
#     git check-ignore -v scripts/tranche-two-launcher.sh   # must still hit
#     git check-ignore -v backend/scripts/backup-jobs.sh    # must exit 1
/scripts/

# flock target from the systemd unit ExecStart -- created on every run
.run.lock

# Per-run volume history, appended by run-daily.py and read by
# tools/volume-check.py. Machine-local operational state, not code: it describes
# what THIS host ingested, so committing it would mean one machine's quiet
# Tuesday becoming another machine's floor. See config/volume-floors.json.
.run-volumes.jsonl

# Dev-only virtualenv for ruff and other lint/CI tooling (T-1 in TASKS.md).
# Deliberately NOT one of the three runtime venvs.
.venv-dev/
